1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Robert Tucker Reviewer Reviewer's Name

2
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:15,960
Hello and welcome to Harvard University.

3
00:00:15,960 --> 00:00:17,840
My name is Michael Sandel.

4
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:21,320
Very soon, Americans will choose a president.

5
00:00:21,320 --> 00:00:24,240
Barack Obama and his challenger Mitt Romney

6
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:26,800
seem to agree on one thing at least.

7
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:29,620
This election offers a fundamental choice

8
00:00:29,620 --> 00:00:33,080
between two different visions of the role of government

9
00:00:33,080 --> 00:00:35,260
and of America's future.

10
00:00:35,260 --> 00:00:37,800
I'd like to invite those of you gathered here,

11
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:40,340
students and members of the general public,

12
00:00:40,340 --> 00:00:43,140
and our radio audience and the BBC

13
00:00:43,140 --> 00:00:46,200
to listen to two contrasting statements

14
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:49,220
about the meaning of individual success

15
00:00:49,220 --> 00:00:52,220
and about who owes what to whom.

16
00:00:57,620 --> 00:00:59,300
If you've been successful,

17
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:02,860
you didn't get there on your own.

18
00:01:02,860 --> 00:01:04,620
You didn't get there on your own.

19
00:01:04,620 --> 00:01:06,979
I'm always struck by people who think,

20
00:01:06,979 --> 00:01:09,580
well, it must be because I was just so smart.

21
00:01:09,580 --> 00:01:12,479
There are a lot of smart people out there.

22
00:01:12,479 --> 00:01:14,780
It must be because I worked harder than everybody else.

23
00:01:14,780 --> 00:01:15,780
Let me tell you something.

24
00:01:15,780 --> 00:01:17,780
There are a whole bunch of hard-working people out there.

25
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:27,420
If you were successful, somebody along the line

26
00:01:27,420 --> 00:01:29,140
gave you some help.

27
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:31,300
There was a great teacher somewhere in your life.

28
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:37,979
Somebody helped to create this unbelievable American system

29
00:01:37,979 --> 00:01:39,979
that we had that allowed you to thrive.

30
00:01:39,979 --> 00:01:42,380
Somebody invested in roads and bridges.

31
00:01:42,380 --> 00:01:45,179
If you've got a business, you didn't build that.

32
00:01:45,179 --> 00:01:47,780
Somebody else made that happen.

33
00:01:47,780 --> 00:01:50,780
I know that there are some people who believe,

34
00:01:50,780 --> 00:01:53,780
and I'm not saying that they're wrong,

35
00:01:53,780 --> 00:01:56,300
but I'm saying that there are some people who believe,

36
00:01:56,300 --> 00:01:57,300
and I'm not saying that they're wrong,

37
00:01:57,300 --> 00:01:58,300
but I'm saying that there are some people who believe,

38
00:01:58,300 --> 00:01:59,300
and I'm not saying that they're wrong,

39
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:00,300
but I'm saying that there are some people who believe,

40
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:01,800
that if you simply take

41
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:03,300
from some and give to others,

42
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:04,300
that we'll all be better off.

43
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:06,300
It's known as redistribution.

44
00:02:06,300 --> 00:02:08,300
It's never been a characteristic of America.

45
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:11,300
Just a tape came out a couple of days ago

46
00:02:11,300 --> 00:02:12,300
with the president saying,

47
00:02:12,300 --> 00:02:14,300
yes, he believes in redistribution.

48
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:16,300
I don't.

49
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:18,300
I believe the way to lift people

50
00:02:18,300 --> 00:02:20,300
and to help people have higher incomes

51
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:22,300
is not to take from some and give to others,

52
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:24,300
but to create wealth for all of us,

53
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:26,300
to create an economy so strong it lifts everybody.

54
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:31,300
This idea of redistribution follows from the idea

55
00:02:31,300 --> 00:02:34,300
that if you have a business, you didn't build it.

56
00:02:34,300 --> 00:02:35,300
Someone else did that.

57
00:02:35,300 --> 00:02:37,300
It's the same concept.

58
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:38,800
See, government is responsible for

59
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:40,300
everything that's going on here,

60
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:44,300
and therefore government can take and give as it chooses.

61
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:47,300
It's an entirely foreign concept that will not work,

62
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:49,300
that has not worked,

63
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:51,300
that has never worked anywhere in the world.

64
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:55,300
Now, the first statement by President Obama

65
00:02:55,300 --> 00:02:59,300
was widely viewed as a gaffe.

66
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:01,300
Republicans have mocked the notion

67
00:03:01,300 --> 00:03:05,300
that the successful owe their success to somebody else

68
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:07,300
or to the government.

69
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:10,300
The second statement by Mitt Romney

70
00:03:10,300 --> 00:03:13,300
came on the heels of a gaffe of its own,

71
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:23,300
a secretly recorded video showing him telling wealthy donors

72
00:03:23,300 --> 00:03:25,300
that 47% of the world's population

73
00:03:25,300 --> 00:03:27,300
of Americans pay no income tax,

74
00:03:27,300 --> 00:03:29,300
are dependent on the government,

75
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:31,300
believe that they are victims,

76
00:03:31,300 --> 00:03:33,300
and believe that they are entitled to health care,

77
00:03:33,300 --> 00:03:36,300
to food, to housing, to you name it.

78
00:03:36,300 --> 00:03:42,300
An astute observer once defined a gaffe

79
00:03:42,300 --> 00:03:45,300
as when a politician inadvertently says

80
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:48,300
what he actually believes.

81
00:03:48,300 --> 00:03:50,300
I'd go further.

82
00:03:50,300 --> 00:03:54,300
These two so-called gaffes actually represent

83
00:03:54,300 --> 00:03:57,300
a rare moment in this campaign

84
00:03:57,300 --> 00:04:01,300
when the two candidates were caught expressing thoughts

85
00:04:01,300 --> 00:04:06,300
that verge on political philosophy.

86
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:09,300
Much of the debate in this campaign

87
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:13,300
revolves around taxes and health care.

88
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:17,300
President Obama favors higher taxes on the wealthy.

89
00:04:17,300 --> 00:04:20,300
Governor Romney favors tax cuts for everyone,

90
00:04:20,300 --> 00:04:22,300
including the most affluent.

91
00:04:22,300 --> 00:04:24,300
And then there is the recent

92
00:04:24,300 --> 00:04:27,300
recently enacted health care reform,

93
00:04:27,300 --> 00:04:29,300
commonly called Obamacare.

94
00:04:29,300 --> 00:04:31,300
Romney wants to repeal it,

95
00:04:31,300 --> 00:04:34,300
Obama wants to keep it.

96
00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:38,300
But lying just beneath the surface of these debates

97
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:42,300
are big questions of political philosophy.

98
00:04:42,300 --> 00:04:44,300
What is a fair society?

99
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:47,300
Who is entitled to what?

100
00:04:47,300 --> 00:04:49,300
And what is the moral significance

101
00:04:49,300 --> 00:04:53,300
of individual success in a market economy?

102
00:04:54,300 --> 00:04:56,300
Let's begin with health care.

103
00:04:56,300 --> 00:04:58,300
Let's put aside the complex details

104
00:04:58,300 --> 00:05:00,300
of the recent health care reform

105
00:05:00,300 --> 00:05:02,300
and focus on a question of principle,

106
00:05:02,300 --> 00:05:05,300
a question about entitlement.

107
00:05:05,300 --> 00:05:10,300
Consider the following statement.

108
00:05:10,300 --> 00:05:14,300
Every American is entitled to decent health care

109
00:05:14,300 --> 00:05:19,300
regardless of his or her ability to pay.

110
00:05:19,300 --> 00:05:23,300
Let's see what people here think about that statement.

111
00:05:23,300 --> 00:05:26,300
Do you agree or do you disagree?

112
00:05:26,300 --> 00:05:28,300
Let's see by a show of hands

113
00:05:28,300 --> 00:05:30,300
how many agree with the statement,

114
00:05:30,300 --> 00:05:33,300
every American is entitled to decent health care

115
00:05:33,300 --> 00:05:36,300
regardless of his or her ability to pay.

116
00:05:36,300 --> 00:05:39,300
Raise your hand if you agree with that statement.

117
00:05:39,300 --> 00:05:42,300
And how many disagree with that statement?

118
00:05:42,300 --> 00:05:48,300
All right, here at Harvard's Kennedy School of Government,

119
00:05:48,300 --> 00:05:50,300
the majority agree with that statement.

120
00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:52,300
A minority disagree.

121
00:05:52,300 --> 00:05:57,300
Let's begin with those who disagree

122
00:05:57,300 --> 00:06:01,300
about everyone being entitled to health care

123
00:06:01,300 --> 00:06:03,300
regardless of their ability to pay.

124
00:06:03,300 --> 00:06:04,300
Why do you disagree?

125
00:06:04,300 --> 00:06:13,300
Who will get our discussion going?

126
00:06:13,300 --> 00:06:15,300
Who else?

127
00:06:15,300 --> 00:06:17,300
Yes.

128
00:06:17,300 --> 00:06:19,300
In the back.

129
00:06:19,300 --> 00:06:20,300
Hey there.

130
00:06:20,300 --> 00:06:22,300
My name is Aaron.

131
00:06:22,300 --> 00:06:25,300
I disagree with this because it implies

132
00:06:25,300 --> 00:06:30,300
if they're entitled to a service or a product

133
00:06:30,300 --> 00:06:31,800
that someone else is going

134
00:06:31,800 --> 00:06:33,300
to be providing that for them.

135
00:06:33,300 --> 00:06:36,300
And I am one of the someone else's.

136
00:06:36,300 --> 00:06:37,800
And I don't feel that I

137
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:39,300
or anyone else should be forced

138
00:06:39,300 --> 00:06:42,300
to have to pay for anyone else's services and products.

139
00:06:42,300 --> 00:06:44,300
You're one of...

140
00:06:49,300 --> 00:06:51,300
Aaron, you're one of the someone else's.

141
00:06:51,300 --> 00:06:58,300
And you don't want to be forced, coerced,

142
00:06:58,300 --> 00:07:00,300
to pay for somebody else's health care.

143
00:07:00,300 --> 00:07:02,300
Yes.

144
00:07:02,300 --> 00:07:04,300
Do you agree?

145
00:07:04,300 --> 00:07:06,300
Well, my name's Andrea.

146
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:07,800
And the point I want

147
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:09,300
to make is, first of all,

148
00:07:09,300 --> 00:07:12,300
I don't understand what decent health care means.

149
00:07:12,300 --> 00:07:14,300
That to me is...

150
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:16,300
I don't know what you're saying,

151
00:07:16,300 --> 00:07:18,300
but I know I come from a family of doctors,

152
00:07:18,300 --> 00:07:20,300
and my sister, her friend,

153
00:07:20,300 --> 00:07:22,300
some of her college friends were very bright,

154
00:07:22,300 --> 00:07:24,300
and they actually worked at Bain Capital with Romney,

155
00:07:24,300 --> 00:07:27,300
and went and made tons of money.

156
00:07:27,300 --> 00:07:29,300
She worked very hard and went to medical school

157
00:07:29,300 --> 00:07:30,800
and slept on a bed

158
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:32,300
that was wrapped up with cords

159
00:07:32,300 --> 00:07:34,300
because she didn't have any money.

160
00:07:34,300 --> 00:07:35,800
And she's now in her 50s

161
00:07:35,800 --> 00:07:37,300
and is finally making some money.

162
00:07:37,300 --> 00:07:39,300
And she's a doctor.

163
00:07:39,300 --> 00:07:41,300
And she's very bright.

164
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:42,300
She's one of the best and the brightest ones

165
00:07:42,300 --> 00:07:43,300
of the best schools.

166
00:07:43,300 --> 00:07:45,300
Not Harvard, but the other medical school

167
00:07:45,300 --> 00:07:48,300
that is often considered the best in the country.

168
00:07:48,300 --> 00:07:49,300
And she doesn't feel like she's the best.

169
00:07:49,300 --> 00:07:52,300
She doesn't feel like she should be punished right now.

170
00:07:52,300 --> 00:07:53,300
And she gives 20...

171
00:07:53,300 --> 00:07:55,300
I think it's more than 20%.

172
00:07:55,300 --> 00:07:58,300
I think she gives 30% of her time for free

173
00:07:58,300 --> 00:07:59,800
as part of the services

174
00:07:59,800 --> 00:08:01,300
that she offers as a doctor.

175
00:08:01,300 --> 00:08:04,300
And she doesn't feel like she should be penalized anymore.

176
00:08:04,300 --> 00:08:06,300
And she's willing to help people,

177
00:08:06,300 --> 00:08:08,300
but why should doctors be punished?

178
00:08:08,300 --> 00:08:10,300
And if you want decent health care,

179
00:08:10,300 --> 00:08:12,300
you have to have decent people.

180
00:08:12,300 --> 00:08:14,300
You know, you want the best and the brightest

181
00:08:14,300 --> 00:08:15,300
to be your brain surgeon

182
00:08:15,300 --> 00:08:16,300
or to be your heart surgeon

183
00:08:16,300 --> 00:08:17,300
or to care for you.

184
00:08:17,300 --> 00:08:19,300
And if you're punishing those people,

185
00:08:19,300 --> 00:08:21,300
you're not going to get those kind of services.

186
00:08:21,300 --> 00:08:22,300
And now why do you think, Andrea...

187
00:08:22,300 --> 00:08:23,300
Decent health care, what does that mean?

188
00:08:23,300 --> 00:08:25,300
Why do you think that it necessarily would involve

189
00:08:25,300 --> 00:08:27,300
punishing doctors?

190
00:08:27,300 --> 00:08:33,299
Suppose the government taxed the taxpayers generally

191
00:08:33,299 --> 00:08:36,299
at a sufficient level to

192
00:08:36,299 --> 00:08:39,299
be able to pay all doctors

193
00:08:39,299 --> 00:08:41,299
a handsome, generous amount.

194
00:08:41,299 --> 00:08:43,299
That wouldn't be punishing doctors.

195
00:08:43,299 --> 00:08:44,300
Would you be for that?

196
00:08:44,300 --> 00:08:45,300
That's not what Obama said.

197
00:08:45,300 --> 00:08:48,300
He said doctors made too much money, didn't he?

198
00:08:48,300 --> 00:08:49,300
He did.

199
00:08:49,300 --> 00:08:51,300
The question is, would you be in favor of that?

200
00:08:51,300 --> 00:08:55,300
If doctors were very well paid by the government,

201
00:08:55,300 --> 00:08:57,300
by the taxpayer to look after everyone's health,

202
00:08:57,300 --> 00:08:59,300
would you be against that in principle?

203
00:08:59,300 --> 00:09:01,300
I'm not against that in principle,

204
00:09:01,300 --> 00:09:02,300
but I don't really think...

205
00:09:02,300 --> 00:09:04,300
Doctors are not trained in nutrition,

206
00:09:04,300 --> 00:09:06,300
and I've had personal experiences in that.

207
00:09:06,300 --> 00:09:08,300
They're good when you're really sick.

208
00:09:08,300 --> 00:09:10,300
They're not good as preventative.

209
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:12,300
Okay, thank you for that.

210
00:09:12,300 --> 00:09:15,300
Who else disagrees with the idea

211
00:09:15,300 --> 00:09:18,300
that every American is entitled

212
00:09:18,300 --> 00:09:22,300
to decent health care

213
00:09:22,300 --> 00:09:25,300
and that the government should tax people

214
00:09:25,300 --> 00:09:27,300
if necessary to provide it?

215
00:09:27,300 --> 00:09:29,300
Who else disagrees with that idea?

216
00:09:32,300 --> 00:09:34,300
Yes.

217
00:09:36,300 --> 00:09:39,300
When we talk about entitlements,

218
00:09:39,300 --> 00:09:42,300
we talk about rights.

219
00:09:42,300 --> 00:09:46,300
And a right to health care

220
00:09:46,300 --> 00:09:48,300
being created

221
00:09:48,300 --> 00:09:50,300
by force

222
00:09:50,300 --> 00:09:52,300
is never a right.

223
00:09:52,300 --> 00:09:56,300
A right cannot be extorted

224
00:09:56,300 --> 00:09:58,300
from somebody else

225
00:09:58,300 --> 00:10:00,300
at the point of a knife

226
00:10:00,300 --> 00:10:02,300
at the...

227
00:10:02,300 --> 00:10:05,300
at the payer's throat.

228
00:10:05,300 --> 00:10:06,300
A knife?

229
00:10:06,300 --> 00:10:08,300
You're not referring to what surgeons do.

230
00:10:08,300 --> 00:10:09,300
Well...

231
00:10:09,300 --> 00:10:11,300
You're talking about the state.

232
00:10:11,300 --> 00:10:13,300
There is a certain point,

233
00:10:13,300 --> 00:10:16,300
but this leads away from the principle.

234
00:10:16,300 --> 00:10:19,300
It leads away from the principle of rights.

235
00:10:19,300 --> 00:10:20,300
All right.

236
00:10:20,300 --> 00:10:21,300
So you would say...

237
00:10:21,300 --> 00:10:22,300
And what's your name?

238
00:10:22,300 --> 00:10:23,300
Klaus.

239
00:10:23,300 --> 00:10:24,300
Klaus.

240
00:10:24,300 --> 00:10:25,800
You would say that there

241
00:10:25,800 --> 00:10:27,300
is no right to health care.

242
00:10:27,300 --> 00:10:30,300
Well, we have to define what is a right.

243
00:10:30,300 --> 00:10:31,300
Well, is there...

244
00:10:31,300 --> 00:10:32,300
Because, you know...

245
00:10:32,300 --> 00:10:34,300
On your definition of a right,

246
00:10:34,300 --> 00:10:36,300
is there a right to health care?

247
00:10:36,300 --> 00:10:37,300
No.

248
00:10:37,300 --> 00:10:40,300
Because a right

249
00:10:40,300 --> 00:10:43,300
is only a right to action.

250
00:10:43,300 --> 00:10:46,300
Read the Declaration of Independence.

251
00:10:46,300 --> 00:10:49,300
There are a few enumerated rights

252
00:10:49,300 --> 00:10:52,300
in the Declaration of Independence.

253
00:10:52,300 --> 00:10:53,300
I remember them.

254
00:10:53,300 --> 00:10:54,300
Which says...

255
00:10:54,300 --> 00:10:55,300
Which says...

256
00:10:55,300 --> 00:10:56,300
Life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness.

257
00:10:56,300 --> 00:10:57,300
Yes.

258
00:10:57,300 --> 00:10:58,300
Yes.

259
00:10:58,300 --> 00:11:01,300
And these are principles of action.

260
00:11:01,300 --> 00:11:03,300
Life is action

261
00:11:03,300 --> 00:11:06,300
because you pursue the values

262
00:11:06,300 --> 00:11:08,300
that sustain your life.

263
00:11:08,300 --> 00:11:09,300
Okay.

264
00:11:09,300 --> 00:11:11,300
Who else takes a similar view?

265
00:11:11,300 --> 00:11:13,300
Go ahead.

266
00:11:13,300 --> 00:11:14,300
All right.

267
00:11:14,300 --> 00:11:16,300
And then we'll come down here.

268
00:11:16,300 --> 00:11:17,300
My name is Jay.

269
00:11:17,300 --> 00:11:18,300
I think my opinion kind

270
00:11:18,300 --> 00:11:19,300
of comes to a similar degree

271
00:11:19,300 --> 00:11:22,300
as the woman who spoke earlier about that coercing someone

272
00:11:22,300 --> 00:11:25,300
to offer service to someone else isn't necessarily fair.

273
00:11:25,300 --> 00:11:29,300
But given your premise that everyone is being paid,

274
00:11:29,300 --> 00:11:32,300
the other concern would be at

275
00:11:32,300 --> 00:11:35,300
what level does decent health care become...

276
00:11:35,300 --> 00:11:37,300
Does everyone have a right to live forever?

277
00:11:37,300 --> 00:11:39,300
Does the government have a right,

278
00:11:39,300 --> 00:11:42,300
like have an obligation to pay for everything forever?

279
00:11:42,300 --> 00:11:44,300
Is that a system that can possibly work

280
